        4
      ],
      "devanagari": "ततः ते राक्षस इन्द्रेण चोदिता मन्त्रिणः सुताः |\nनिर्ययुः भवनात् तस्मात् सप्त सप्त अर्चि वर्चसः\nमहाबल परीवारा धनुष्मन्तो महाबलाः |\nकृत अस्त्रा अस्त्रविदाम् श्रेष्ठाः परस्पर जय एषिणः\nहेम जाल परिक्षिप्तैः ध्वजवद्भिः पताकिभिः |\nतोयद स्वन निर्घोषैः वाजि युक्तैः महारथैः\nतप्त कान्चन चित्राणि चापानि अमित विक्रमाः |\nविस्फारयन्तः सम्हृष्टाः तडिद्वन्त इव अम्बुदाः",
      "iast": "tataḥ te rākṣasa indreṇa coditā mantriṇaḥ sutāḥ |\nniryayuḥ bhavanāt tasmāt sapta sapta arci varcasaḥ\nmahābala parīvārā dhanuṣmanto mahābalāḥ |\nkṛta astrā astravidām śreṣṭhāḥ paraspara jaya eṣiṇaḥ\nhema jāla parikṣiptaiḥ dhvajavadbhiḥ patākibhiḥ |\ntoyada svana nirghoṣaiḥ vāji yuktaiḥ mahārathaiḥ\ntapta kāncana citrāṇi cāpāni amita vikramāḥ |\nvisphārayantaḥ samhṛṣṭāḥ taḍidvanta iva ambudāḥ",
      "translation": "Thereafter, those seven sons of his chief minister having a lustre similar to that of fire, surrounded by a great army, wielding their respective bows, possessing a great strength, well-trained in archery and the best among knower of weapons, wishing a victory reciprocally, having an immense prowess each, and as directed by Ravana, started highly delighted from that palace, in great chariots yoked with horses. Those chariots were having a noise similar to that of an autumnal cloud, overlaid with a protective armour of gold, surmounted by banners and decorated with smaller flags. They were stretching their bows inlaid with refined gold and looking colourful like clouds accompanied by flashes of lightning.",
